Shape-keyed CUDA graph slots, capture gate, slot cap - mm7, the engine
half of fixed-shape speculative decoding.

Revives the mm4 experiment with the two guards its failure taught, and
in a changed world: since the mm5 revert, host-side topk sparse logits
killed the guest-side verify-sampling tax that the revert named as a
dominant term, and p_min=0 fixed-k drafts (llm-chat >= 0.30) make every
verify pass the SAME shape, which is the one workload shape-keying is
built for. Measured on fable-fusion-27b (dense qwen35, 2026-08-01):
every multi-token pass pays a flat ~40-47ms (batches 2-8) vs a 15.5ms
replayed 1-token step - a fixed un-graphed-launch tax under
confidential compute, not per-token compute.

Change 1 (get_key): fold the shape signature (node count + first/last
node extents) into the graph-map key so each RECURRING ubatch shape
warms and replays its own slot instead of resetting one shared slot on
every alternation. GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_SHAPES=0 restores the upstream key
(kill-switch, host env).

Change 2 (capture gate): capture only after GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_WARMUP
consecutive property-stable calls on a slot (default 3). Rare shapes
and colliding shapes never capture - direct execution, stock behavior.
This was mm4's fix for the mm3 churn regression.

Change 3 (slot cap, new in mm7): at most GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_SLOTS slots
(default 6) may ever hold a captured graph per context; later shapes
are created but never capture. mm4 still regressed k16/p0.3 (39 -> 32)
because 17+ recurring draft lengths each eventually captured; the cap
bounds that failure mode by construction while leaving fixed-shape
configs ({1-token, verify k+1, a couple of re-feeds}) untouched.

